{"term":"肛管","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gāngguǎn","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anal_canal","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The short terminal segment of the large intestine connecting the rectum to the anal opening; standard clinical anatomical term in Mandarin medicine.","english_term":"anal canal","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Pure medical/anatomical term; rarely used outside clinical or educational contexts."}
{"term":"菊花","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"júhuā","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'chrysanthemum flower'; the most widespread Mandarin slang for the anus, derived from the visual resemblance of the flower's radial petals to the anal sphincter.","english_term":"ass","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Appears in compounds such as 爆菊 (to have anal sex). One of the most culturally embedded slang terms for the anus in modern Mandarin."}
{"term":"屁眼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"pìyǎn","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'fart hole'; a widely used crude Mandarin slang term for the anus.","english_term":"asshole","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"屁 means flatulence; 眼 means eye or hole. Also deployed as a personal insult parallel to English 'asshole'."}
{"term":"屁股眼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"pìgu yǎn","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'butt hole'; crude Mandarin slang for the anus, compounding 屁股 (buttocks) with 眼 (hole).","english_term":"asshole","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More explicit than 屁眼 by including the full 屁股 (butt) root; used interchangeably in crude registers."}
{"term":"后庭","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"hòutíng","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'back courtyard'; a literary euphemism for the anus, historically embedded in classical Chinese erotic literature.","english_term":"back passage","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Source of the classical erotic phrase 后庭花 (anal sex). Also the title of a famous poem by the Chen dynasty ruler Chen Shubao, adding a layer of literary connotation; recognition may be lower among younger speakers."}
{"term":"后门","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"hòumén","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":28,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'back door'; a widely understood Mandarin euphemism for the anus in sexual contexts.","english_term":"back door","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"High potential for ambiguity: 走后门 means to use bribery or nepotism. Context reliably disambiguates the sexual meaning."}
{"term":"菊","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jú","category":"organs","subcategory":"anus","concept":"anus","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clipped form of 菊花 (chrysanthemum); abbreviated internet and colloquial slang for the anus.","english_term":"ass","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Especially prevalent in internet slang and BL (boys' love) fan communities. Appears in 爆菊 (anal penetration) and 菊部 (anal region)."}
{"term":"睾丸","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gāowán","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard clinical Mandarin term for the testicles, the male gonads responsible for sperm and testosterone production.","english_term":"testicles","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used in medical, educational, and public health contexts. Rarely used in everyday or sexual conversation where slang terms dominate."}
{"term":"阴囊","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"yīnnáng","category":"organs","subcategory":"scrotum","concept":"scrotum","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard clinical Mandarin term for the scrotum, the external skin pouch enclosing and protecting the testicles.","english_term":"scrotum","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Less commonly recognized by the general public than 睾丸; primarily used in clinical and educational settings."}
{"term":"蛋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dàn","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'egg'; the most common everyday Mandarin slang for testicle(s), mirroring the English metaphor 'ball'.","english_term":"ball","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primary meaning is 'egg' (food). Generates compound insults: 混蛋 (bastard), 王八蛋 (son of a bitch). One of the most culturally embedded terms in Mandarin."}
{"term":"蛋蛋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dàndàn","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Reduplicated form of 蛋 (egg); affectionate or childlike slang for testicles, slightly softer in register than plain 蛋.","english_term":"balls","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Reduplication in Mandarin typically connotes diminutive or affectionate tone; widely used in both internet and everyday colloquial speech."}
{"term":"卵","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"luǎn","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'egg/ovum'; crude slang for testicles in mainland Mandarin (and penis in Taiwanese/Southern dialect usage), also functions as a general crude dismissive expletive.","english_term":"balls","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In mainland Mandarin, primarily testicles; in Taiwanese Hokkien-influenced usage, more often penis. Also a general crude dismissive (我卵才管 ≈ 'I don't give a shit'). Has a classical Chinese neutral meaning of 'egg.'"}
{"term":"卵蛋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"luǎndàn","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":73,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Compound of two slang terms for testicles (卵 + 蛋); a crude, semantically emphatic designation for the testicles.","english_term":"balls","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The compound is redundant by design, for emphasis. Less frequent than either component alone; more common in Southern Chinese dialects."}
{"term":"两颗","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"liǎng kē","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'two pieces/grains' (using the classifier 颗 for small round objects); a purely deictic euphemism for the testicles that avoids naming them directly.","english_term":"the two","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Entirely context-dependent; without surrounding context refers to any two small round objects. 颗 is the standard classifier for pills, seeds, and pearls — the choice of classifier implies round, small anatomy. Lowest crude score in this set due to total indirection."}
{"term":"蛋疼","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dàn téng","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"blue_balls","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'egg/ball ache'; primarily used on the Chinese internet as a crude expletive meaning something is annoying, pointless, or frustrating; secondarily refers to literal testicular discomfort.","english_term":"ball ache","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Mapped to blue_balls as the closest anatomical concept, but dominant modern usage is a general frustration expletive (≈ English 'pain in the ass'), not specifically post-arousal testicular discomfort. Became a major internet meme circa 2010–2015."}
{"term":"乳房","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rǔfáng","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard clinical and literary Mandarin term for the female breast, used in medical, educational, and formal written contexts.","english_term":"breast","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"乳 relates to milk/lactation (cf. 母乳 = breast milk); 房 means room or chamber. Neutral and formal; not used in casual or sexual conversation."}
{"term":"乳头","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rǔtóu","category":"organs","subcategory":"nipple","concept":"nipple","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":82,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Mandarin term for the nipple; used in both clinical and everyday contexts, with moderate crude potential in sexual contexts.","english_term":"nipple","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'milk head.' Crosses clinical and everyday registers more fluidly than 乳房 — it is the default term with no common crude substitute in widespread use."}
{"term":"乳晕","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rǔyūn","category":"organs","subcategory":"areola","concept":"areola","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Mandarin term for the areola, the pigmented circular ring of skin surrounding the nipple.","english_term":"areola","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primarily used in medical, dermatological, and obstetric contexts. Less widely recognized outside educational or health contexts."}
{"term":"胸部","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xiōngbù","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":8,"technical":70,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'chest region'; the standard neutral term for the chest, widely used as a polite euphemism for female breasts in everyday and media contexts.","english_term":"chest","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Anatomically refers to the thorax in both sexes; in female-specific contexts defaults to meaning breasts. Preferred in formal and public-facing media over crude terms."}
{"term":"奶子","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nǎizi","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":74,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang for female breasts; one of the most common vulgar terms for breasts in Mandarin Chinese.","english_term":"tits","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from 奶 (milk) + nominalizer 子. Ubiquitous in erotic literature, internet discourse, and crude speech. Functionally equivalent to English 'tits' in register and vulgarity."}
{"term":"奶","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nǎi","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clipped form of 奶子; slang for female breasts; also the standard Mandarin word for milk — context determines the active meaning.","english_term":"boobs","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is 'milk/dairy' (牛奶 = cow's milk; 奶茶 = milk tea). Sexual meaning activated entirely by context. Less crude than 奶子 due to the everyday milk association softening the register."}
{"term":"咪咪","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"mīmī","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":32,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":84,"recognition_pct_peak":84,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Reduplicated childlike slang for female breasts; the soft sound evokes cuteness and a mildly coy rather than aggressively sexual register.","english_term":"boobies","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"咪咪 also represents the sound a cat makes (meow). The dual meaning (cute/cat sound + breasts) reinforces the playful, infantilizing register. Used in flirtatious or teasing contexts more than in explicit sexual speech."}
{"term":"波","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"bō","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":76,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":2005,"regional":"HK","definition":"Cantonese-origin slang for female breasts (Cantonese: 波 bō, 'ball/wave'), recognized across Mandarin media via Hong Kong cultural influence.","english_term":"boobs","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In Mandarin, 波 means 'wave'; the breast meaning is purely Cantonese-derived. Crossed into mainland awareness via Hong Kong films, TV, and internet. Recognition peaked during peak Hong Kong media influence on mainland China (roughly 1990s–2000s)."}
{"term":"大波","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"dà bō","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breasts","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":73,"recognition_pct_peak":79,"peak_year":2005,"regional":"HK","definition":"Literally 'big waves/balls'; Cantonese-origin slang specifically for large breasts, recognized across Mandarin contexts via Hong Kong media.","english_term":"big boobs","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Compound of 大 (big) + 波 (Cantonese breast slang). Appears in 大波妹 (busty girl). Recognition follows the same trajectory as 波 — tied to the Hong Kong cultural export era."}
{"term":"胸","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xiōng","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'chest'; the most versatile Mandarin term for the chest/breast area, used neutrally for anatomy in both sexes and as a mild euphemism for female breasts in sexualized contexts.","english_term":"chest","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Unlike 胸部, the bare morpheme 胸 skews younger and more internet-native in sexualized usage (胸大 = big-chested; 胸控 = breast fetishist). Lower crude than 奶子 or 奶 because it is also the default clinical/everyday word for chest."}
